 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T07:01+08:00",
 "answer": "No, the haze in Johor Bahru is generally comparable to or slightly better than what Singapore has been experiencing, though both areas are dealing with significant, unhealthy air quality driven by regional smoke. \n\n* **Singapore:** Air quality has reached severe levels, with parts of the island crossing into the **\"Very Unhealthy\"** range (24-hour PSI readings climbing past 200, peaking over 220 in some areas). Singapore has faced a prolonged stretch of unhealthy air due to prevailing winds carrying smoke from Sumatra and Kalimantan. \n* **Johor Bahru:** Air quality metrics (such as the Air Quality Index/AQI) typically register in the **Unhealthy** range (roughly 170–200), closely mirroring the southern regions of Peninsular Malaysia and Singapore. \n\nBecause they are geographically right next to each other and affected by the same regional wind patterns blowing smoke from Indonesian land and forest fires, both cities suffer from thick regional haze. However, specific localized readings fluctuate by the hour depending on shifting wind directions and localized showers, meaning Singapore's worst-hit regions have occasionally tracked higher peak numbers than JB.",
